r  in a co2 incubator but  different manufacturers have applied  this  with different varying efficiencies so  if i hold a hepa filter in my hand it's  not going to filter  the air in my room you need to have  forced air  through that hepa filter in order to  clean it it's not a passive system it's  an  active system so a good hepa filtration  system will filter the entire  incubator air volume every 60 seconds  providing iso class 5 clean room  conditions only 5 minutes after  a 30 second door opening  now some manufacturers will say yeah we  have hepa filters on our incubator we  have  five or seven hepa filters they're  actually calling that gas line air  filter a hepa filter and that's not true  a hepa filter is three-dimensional and  you should look for one that's actually  in the incubator chamber  protecting the air that your cells are  experiencing  if the hepa filter is in the electronics  compartment and you have to use  tools to change it that's probably not  the best design  okay i'm going to finish up talking  about on-demand  types of contamination control in the  incubator  and there's just a few technologies left  to talk about the first is dry heat  sterilization  and this can be confusing because years  ago the international pharmacopoeias  would  date a given time and temperature so you  had to have  160 degrees for two hours and that was  sterilization  or 140 degrees for two and a half hours  and that was sterilization  and so manufacturers would say okay  check mark we've got 160 degrees for two  hours  we provide sterilization and it turned  out that some areas of those sterilizers  or incubators  never reached 160 degrees so in  some areas of that chamber  microorganisms  survived so now if the international  pharmacopeias require  proof of efficacy using accepted  designated heat resistant bacterial  spores  and you have to prove elimination of 1  million microorganisms  and also there's a requirement to  circulate that heated air using a fa