e  presentation  i'm going to begin with talking about  what is cell culture contamination  including the types of contamination  and the extent of the problem so i think  it's important that we all begin  at the same place and so really  contamination is  anything that infects the culture or  makes it  impure so with a better understanding of  what is  what are the different types of cell  culture contamination then we can look  at  recommendations to reduce contamination  and then the second half of the talk  will be  looking more closely at different co2  incubator designs that  help to fight contamination in the  incubator  that's important because the cell  culture incubator is where your cells  spend most of their time so i would  argue that it's the most important lab  equipment  for for keeping yourself healthy and  then i'll finish up with some  conclusions  so let's get into talking about the  types of cell culture contamination and  we'll start with viruses  the smallest types of cell culture  contamination  we i think we're all thinking about  viruses a lot globally right now  so what do you do if you have a virus in  your cell culture  the thing about viruses is you can't see  them under a regular microscope and they  don't  cause a ph indicator change in your  growth medium  so you you may not know that you have a  virus in your culture  theoretically you could use antiviral  drugs to to limit  viruses growing in your cultures but  those drugs  can also be toxic to the host itself so  that's  generally not a good idea the good news  about viruses  is like the common cold or even like the  sars covid  ii virus that everyone's so  concerned about right now those viruses  tend to be self-limiting on an  individual basis  so with regard to viruses the major  concern is the potential risk to you  working with those cell cultures that  could be harboring a latent virus  that's a special concern if you're  working with human cells  so it's important with that