ou don't need to use an  additional disinfectant so it saves you  time  it works 24 7 and it's actually safe for  cells because your cells  it's not airborne it only kills on  contact so your cells and culture dishes  are safe  this is a quote from a pi i talked with  a few years ago  he was so excited about his copper  incubators once they switched to copper  they just had no contamination  and the lab next door their stainless  steel incubators was still fighting  contamination  um and they didn't have to clean the  incubator as often  he felt the copper was really saving  them time and money  so how does copper work well a few years  back the u.s environmental protection  agency  tested more than 300 different alloys of  copper and they found that you have to  have at least  60 copper in order to have any  antimicrobial  antimicrobial activity so this is  a picture showing testing with different  alloys of copper  and testing um what is that  50 million uh methicillin-resistant  staphylococcus aureus so  mrsa bacteria that are antibiotic  resistant  and they found that on 99 to 100 copper  all of those bacteria were eliminated in  90 minutes it was probably even faster  but that was the first time point they  looked at and as you reduce the copper  content you can see that 80 percent  copper takes  much longer to kill all those bacteria  and as you go down to 55  copper it never kills all of them and  stainless steel had no effect  so only a 100 copper will give you  maximum  efficiency on copper now  copper is a naturally occurring element  and it reacts with everything and one of  the things that it reacts with is copper  as uh  oxygen and so copper develops a natural  tarnish or patina just like  good quality brass which is mostly  copper or good quality silver will do  so this test shows the effectiveness of  the tarnish the tarnish actually makes  the copper  more effective so here this group has  taken  the one centimeter squares of copper of  different alloys  and tested them with 50 million e coli  o157 h7 one  one version of e coli that causes food  poisoning and they found  that the 99 copper does have some effect  when tested over the course of an  hour but not that much more than 90  percent copper  and then they took these same squares of  copper and put them outdoors in new york  city for one year and let them develop  a really strong tarnish or patina they  looked blue and  black and green and then they  disinfected them and added the e coli  again  and look what happens when that copper  gets oxidized it's much more effective  so in the incubator the copper also is  going to get black and green  it's going to develop that kind of  tarnish that's not something to be  afraid of because tarnished copper is  much more effective  than bright copper  i'm going to talk about a few more  technologies to help fight  contamination in the incubator and the  next one is hepa filtration  and i'm going to talk about this because  like copper it's a common misconception  about how  hepa filters work hepa filters actually  do capture  all particles circulating in the air  regardless of their size  and this is confusing because hepa  filters are rated for their efficiency  of capturing 0.3 micrometer particles  and it's confusing because if you think  about gas line  air filters those disk filters on the  back of your incubator  that filter the incoming gases those are  also  0.3 micrometer but in that case it's  talking about the pore  size is 0.3 micrometer so it's not  capturing  smaller particles that's not true with a  hepa filter a hepa filter  is it's an efficiency rating for 0.3  micrometers and you can see on this  chart that what happens is the 0.3  micrometer particles  kind of fall between the different  physical processes  that are active in capturing those  particles  so smaller and part and larger particles  are actually captured with higher  efficiency in a hepa filter  so it's a good idea to put a hepa filter  in a co2 incubator but  different manufacturers have applied  this  with different varying efficiencies so  if i hold a hepa filter in my hand it's  not going to filter  the air in my room you need to have  forced air  through that hepa filter in order to  clean it it's not a passive system it's  an  active system so a good hepa filtration  system will filter the entire  incubator air volume every 60 seconds  providing iso class 5 clean room  conditions only 5 minutes after  a 30 second door opening  now some manufacturers will say yeah we  have hepa filters on our incubator we  have  five or seven hepa filters they're  actually calling that gas line air  filter a hepa filter and that's not true  a hepa filter is three-dimensional and  you should look for one that's actually  in the incubator chamber  protecting the air that your cells are  experiencing  if the hepa filter is in the electronics  compartment and you have to use  tools to change it that's probably not  the best design  okay i'm going to finish up talking  about on-demand  types of contamination control in the  incubator  and there's just a few technologies left  to talk about the first is dry heat  sterilization  and this can be confusing because years  ago the international pharmacopoeias  would  date a given time and temperature so you  had to have  160 degrees for two hours and that was  sterilization  or 140 degrees for two and a half hours  and that was sterilization  and so manufacturers would say okay  check mark we've got 160 degrees for two  hours  we provide sterilization and it turned  out that some areas of those sterilizers  or incubators  never reached 160 degrees so in  some areas of that chamber  microorganisms  survived so now if the international  pharmacopeias require  proof of efficacy using accepted  designated heat resistant bacterial  spores  and you have to prove elimination of 1  million microorganisms  and also there's a requirement to  circulate that heated air using a fan  or a blower so in short these  manufacturers now according to the  pharmacopeias have to provide proof  of sterilization proof of the efficacy  of their design  so here's an example of something to  look for for sterilization proof  in this case this incubator  eliminates all of these microorganisms  tested including  the approved indicator for dry heat  sterilization bacillus atrophies or  bacillus suddenly  and also the approved indicator for  autoclave sterilization the geobacillus  and you can see the number of  microorganisms that were tested  clearly for those two last species  there's between a million and 10 million  tested uh you can see that no growth was  discovered and  this this the the log reduction here is  shown as well  in this case this test was done with a  45 minute cycle at 180 degrees  for a six log reduction and then that  treatment was doubled  using the u.s pharmacopoeia overkill  approach  so the cycle actually heats to 180  degrees for 90 minutes for an effective  12  log sterility assurance level  another thing to look for when  considering dry heat sterilization  is a temperature map so all areas of  that incubator actually reach and hold  at the specified temperature or the  specified  amount of time so in this case here's  one example of what to look for this is  a 48-point temperature map  showing that all areas reach at least  180 degrees for at least 90 minutes  that assures you that all areas truly do  reach that temperature  and there are no cold spots where  microorganisms could survive  okay two last things to talk about for a  contamination features  of of an incubator the first is  ultraviolet light  so ultraviolet light you're actually  hearing more about now with regard to  ways to control the sarskova ii  coronavirus  and ultraviolet light has long been used  in biological safety cabinets  um now there's the thing about uv light  is it requires a certain intensity  of the light for a certain time so it's  about time  and exposure okay so for example if i  just  walk from my office to my car i probably  won't get a sunburn  but if i sit outside in the sun for two  hours i probably will get a sunburn so  it's about the intensity  and the duration of exposure to the uv  light  so with this in mind some manufacturers  have started to offer uv light  to decontaminate the air in an incubator  and this seems like a great idea we use  it in the bsc  but it turns out that it's one of those  ideas that in practice doesn't work so  great  part of it is that an incubator has a  lot of parts that will block that uv  light and  areas in shadow are not disinfected  most importantly in the incubator  most incubators for cell culture have a  humidity level  of 80 to 95 percent so it's very humid  and that air is about five percent  water vapor and it turns out that that  water vapor  is reducing the effectiveness or  blocking the uv light in the incubator  so while uv light works nice in a  large uh empty biosafety cabinet  in an incubator with a bunch of parts  that can block the light and but most  importantly with that high humidity  it's just not very effective also the  bulb  effectiveness decreases with age as  those bulbs get older the light  intensity decreases so  it's important to know even in the bsc  how old is your uv light so that you  know when to change it so that it  actually will be effective  okay the last technology i'm going to  talk about is hydrogen peroxide vapor  so hydrogen peroxide is a really  effective broad spectrum disinfectant  and it's actually been tested and  approved by the u.s environmental aid  prac in u.s environmental protection  agency  as an approved disinfectant but only  a specific type of  of automated uh manual i'm sorry  machine produced hydrogen peroxide it  has to be 35 percent  it has to be performed by professionals  using a machine as pictured  here and what happens is the hydrogen  peroxide  liquid is vaporized it kills the  microorganisms on contact  now reputable company will test and show  the effectiveness they will show that no  microorganisms survived  and they will also guarantee that they  neutralize this toxic chemical which  otherwise can pose a risk  to lab workers for disinfecting a co2  incubator  there are some companies now that are  offering dry non-condensing hydrogen  peroxide and that's  the best approach because it's not going  to cause corrosion in the incubator  and affect the electronics  now someone said to themselves you know  hydrogen peroxide is a really effective  disinfectant let's  automate it in a co2 incubator and that  sounds like a great idea but it turns  out in practice it's hard to  really do effectively because when  you're  automating it it's really hard to  completely distribute those molecules  around  every area of the incubator and so any  areas that  don't make contact with that hydrogen  peroxide  droplets microorganisms can  survive also you have to take out all of  the parts of the incubator and  reposition them  so that it doesn't block spreading of  that vapor phase hydrogen peroxide  and then after the cycle is over you  have to pull out all of those parts and  put them back into place  again so it's actually a lot of work for  you  it doesn't save any time over just  you know manually cleaning and  disinfecting your incubator yourself and  autoclaving the parts  and then finally you see that there is a  puddle at the end of this  35 hydrogen peroxide which is really  toxic for  you and toxic for your cells so it's  again it's more  more work for you and it's a little bit  of danger for the user and then also you  have to continue to purchase that  specific  concentration of hydrogen peroxide and  the cost for that add up  so when you're looking at different  features for contamination control in a  co2 incubator  look for independent proof are the tests  to show the efficacy done by independent  test labs not by the manufacturer  what's involved in that process for you  does it actually require a lot of time  for the people in your lab to perform  that process  could you repeat the experiment when you  look at the marketing brochure are the  graphs clearly labeled are the axes  labeled do you know what they're  measuring or is it just kind of a pretty  dramatic graph and you don't exactly  know what it's  what it's telling you and if you look  for a table of microorganisms tested and  eliminated  is it just a list of zeros or do you  know how many microorganisms they  started with did they really start with  1 million  or did they only start with 50 for  example  and do all areas reach the specified  temperature  do you see a temperature map showing you  that everybody reaches those  every place in that incubator reaches  that specified temperature  okay so just finishing up with a few  words about thermoscientific incubators  because um you know they sent me here  so um thermoscientific incubator  thermoscientific has been making co2  incubators for more than 60 years  and many of the features available on  co2 incubators today were first  introduced by thermoscientific  so we actually have a few new models of  incubators that were introduced in the  last few years that have new  technologies  including redesigned airflow cutting  edge co2 sensors  covered water reservoir that also heats  and humidifies the gases  so better contributing to fast recovery  after a door opening  proven sterilization um options for  variable oxygen  oxygen control and all of these  technologies are have been tested by  third party test labs  finally i mentioned a quarantine  incubator early in the talk  one option for that is a new design from  thermoscientific  our cell locker system where a standard  incubator has been subdivided into these  six  polycarbonate boxes each of which holds  nine t75 flasks or up to 28 micro  uh microwell plates and it's filtered  air  so that no microorganisms could enter  and leave so if you're working with stem  cells or primary cells  and you want to protect those in a  shared incubator this is a good option  if your lab is starting to work on  testing coronaviruses  this is a good way to to isolate  different viral clones for example  okay i want to save a little bit of time  for questions so just to summarize  the take-home points here cell culture  contamination is a continuing challenge  for  everybody and it includes bacteria  including mycoplasma  fungi viruses but also  cross-contamination  from other cell lines and volatile  organic compounds  that also pose a risk to your cells we  all know that contamination takes a lot  of time and money can even  affect your reputation prevention of  contamination  requires doing all the things you know  you're supposed to do  including maintaining your good  technique  in the co2 incubator contamination in  any incubator comes from outside the  incubator  and there are good ways to prevent  contamination in the incubator  cleaning regularly or 100  copper or hepa filtration when you're  looking at these different methods  look at the data provided can you repeat  the experiment do you clearly understand  what they're showing  and ultraviolet light and automated  hydrogen peroxide are not really  effective solutions for controlling  contamination in the incubator and all  of this is important because everything  that you do  depends on your cells growing in a  healthy fashion  we do have a lot of technical resources  available at our website at  thermofisher.com  co2 and that's the end thanks everybody  for your attention  i guess i have some time to take a few  questions  and thank you mary kay for that  informative presentation  and we will start the live q a portion  of this webinar and if you have any  questions you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on that ask  a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time  for so let's take a look at our  questions already coming in  and what a great audience we have today  we already have so many questions  ready for you mary kay our first  question is  is biological containment popularly  used to mitigate the accidental release  of microorganisms that can infect  a human okay  so i'm not entirely sure what the  question is about biological containment  um so i think if we're talking about  biological containment  generally in the laboratory we're  talking about the bio safety cabinet  it's really important to  it's really important to use a biosafety  cabinet that's been properly balanced  and certified because  as those hepa filters load up  that can can that can affect how the air  flow  protects not only your cultured cells  but also  you as a laboratory worker if you're  especially if you're working with  human cells so um and and i guess  in the incubator another way to consider  biological containment is that cell  locker system i just talked about  thank you mary kay our next audience  member says  we're getting recurrent mold growth  in primary cell culture and we have also  been noticing  slow growth and growth in patches  can mold cause these growth issues  also or suggestions for eradicating  mold do you have any  well mold is always a tough one because  it circulates in the laboratory air  and primary cells are particularly  difficult because they're coming from  an animal or you know human cells and so  they're bringing their own normal flora  with them  so uh that might be a situation where  you want to use an anti-fungal in the  growth medium  and then also really really clean the  laboratory  and look at the ceiling air ducts in the  lab and see  is that blowing contaminated air around  the lab or onto the incubator  also think about where is the biosafety  cabinet positioned  is it in an area where you have a lot of  traffic walking back and forth in front  of the bsc because  that can affect how airflow enters the  bsc  um and so you might be having fun fungi  coming in  into the bsc with traffic and strange  airflow patterns inside the bsc so it's  better to put the bsd in a corner  rather than right next to a doorway for  example  and then the only other advice i have at  this  point is really clean the lab and try to  reduce the amount of circulating fungi  in the lab  and proper proper aseptic technique of  course um  if you want to send me an email we might  be able to talk more about this offline  thank you mary kay and in a multi-well  plate  should one discard all of the cells when  only one of the  wells is contaminated in a multi-well  plate  i would say yeah i would discard that  whole plate  theoretically they are all separated but  i  i'm guessing you started with the same  with the with the single culture  distributed across that plate so i would  discard that whole plate  thank you and again i want to thank our  audience members for these fantastic  questions coming in  our next question what is the best state  to leave an  empty co2 incubator between cultures  um could you repeat the question susie i  missed a word there what is the best  sure no problem what is the best feat or  way to leave an  empty co2 incubator between  cultures okay so  what's the best state to leave the co2  incubator okay um  so it depends on how long you're you're  leaving that co2 incubator if it's for a  week or more i would probably  clean it thoroughly and shut it down  turn off the co2 gas  empty out the water and that sort of  thing so for example if your lab has  shut down during the pandemic  you want to clean and disinfect it dry  it out  completely and turn off all of the  settings including the co2 gas  if it's a short term um i would still  so for example if you're between  projects and you're switching from  ending one project and starting another  one i would definitely clean and  disinfect  that incubator and start with clean  sterilized distilled water in the water  pan  thank you so much our next question  please let me know why most labs don't  prefer copy copper incubator and is  there any specific reason for that  i guess i would quibble with the idea  that most labs don't prefer  copper thermoscientific has been making  copper line incubators for more than 40  years and i  think we're up to 65 000  copper units produced over those years  so  a lot of people love copper but if i  were to give one reason why people would  prefer stainless steel i think it's  because you kind of get used to  stainless open the incubator and the  stainless steel is all shiny  and you feel like oh man that is really  clean  and then you if you look at a copper  incubator as i said it's  it's going to naturally develop  black and blue colors um and it just  doesn't look as clean so what i tell  people is close the incubator door you  shouldn't be using it as a mirror  that's where your cells are growing and  the copper is protecting your cells  but there are definitely some people who  are always going to prefer stainless  steel and that's okay too  thank you mary kay and thank you  audience members i just want to remind  everyone that any questions not asked  answered today will be answered via  email  would you like to provide any final  comments for our audience  okay before we close  um i guess i would just say to everyone  stay safe  and best wishes for the safety of your  family and yourselves  and um good luck with your work um  and contamination is is something we all  battle with so  um just try and do all of the things  that your mom would tell you to do  in the laboratory  thank you again mary kay bates for this  great quest  presentation today i also want to thank  thermo fisher scientific for sponsoring  today's webinar i'd also like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions and  questions we did not have time for today  again  those submitted during on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via  contact information  you provided at the time of registration  thank you everyone and have a great day  thank you susie
 

